<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Helix Dynamics — MCF Perseverance Incident</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9010-whistleblower.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player compiled their original anomaly data and submitted a full technical account to the IESB incident investigation?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9009-incident-report-aftermath-gravity-accountability</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      The message arrived at 06:14 station time on a Tuesday. A one-line
      alert in the Helix Dynamics internal news feed: "MCF Perseverance —
      drive anomaly reported — vessel operating on backup systems —
      crew safe." By 08:00 it was on the public feeds. By 09:00 the
      Helix Dynamics share price had moved. By 10:00 you had received
      a message from Legal. By 10:15 you had received a message from
      Dr. Freya Andersen at the IESB. Her message was seven words:
      "I need to speak with you. Today."
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      An in-service drive failure on a crewed Colonial Fleet vessel
      is a Tier 1 incident under IESB regulations. An automatic
      investigation is mandatory. All documentation related to the
      drive's qualification programme is now evidence. This includes
      your original anomaly report, the board's findings, and every
      signature in the test log. The Legal message advised you not
      to speak to anyone about MJOLNIR without counsel present.
      The IESB message doesn't have that restriction. You have to
      choose which call to return first.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      Your workstation, Lab 9. The telemetry window you opened three
      months ago showing the Perseverance's drive data is still open.
      It is now flagged red and showing the drive status as OFFLINE —
      SAFE MODE. The crew status reads: 14 crew, all accounted for.
      The backup ion drive is running. The vessel is 40 light-minutes
      from the nearest relay station. They are not in danger. This
      time.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      The drive failed. The anomaly you found in Cycle 7 — the resonance
      pattern that ARIA modelled and the board cleared as a one-time
      artifact — recurred at 847 operating hours under full service load.
      The confinement coil cracked. The failsafe triggered as designed.
      The crew is safe. The drive is destroyed. The investigation will
      determine whether the board's finding was wrong, whether the
      data was inadequate, or whether the design had a flaw that
      qualified testing should have caught. Your name is in the record.
      You reported the anomaly. You did what you were supposed to do.
      Whether that will be enough depends on what the investigation finds.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Dr. Freya Andersen (IESB): The investigator. She was on the original
      board. She cleared the drive. She is now re-examining that decision
      and she needs your technical account. She is not hostile. She is
      thorough, and she is not going to let this be clean if it isn't clean.

      Legal Counsel (Obi Mwangi): Back. More serious this time. Genuinely
      trying to protect you as well as the company — those interests align
      right now. He wants to see your original data before you talk to anyone.

      Dr. Priya Vasquez: Has not messaged you. That is, in itself, information.

      Tomás Engel: Messaged at 08:43: "are you okay. i saw the news."
      He is the only person today who has asked that.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>Lab 9, Ring 2, Helix Dynamics Orbital Yard</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        The drive failed in service. Crew safe. Investigation opened.
        Legal and IESB are both waiting for your call. You have the
        original anomaly data. You have your notes from the night
        of Cycle 7. You reported correctly. The board cleared it.
        The drive failed anyway.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        You were right, in a way that the process was also right,
        and both of those things are true, and 14 people are alive
        on backup power 40 light-minutes away. Now you do the next
        right thing. You've been doing the next right thing since
        Cycle 7. Keep going.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      This is the hardest world in the series. The player did everything
      correctly and the outcome was still bad — not catastrophic, but bad.
      The tone should not be punishing. It should be honest. Nobody is
      a villain. The board made a reasonable decision with limited data.
      The player reported accurately. The drive failed anyway. That
      happens. The question now is purely procedural and ethical:
      give the investigation everything you have, clearly and without
      self-protection. The crew is safe. That is not nothing.
      Hold on to that.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
